		<h3 class="accordion-header faq-question-header">
			<button class="accordion-button gap-3 z-0" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
				data-bs-target="#question-137-answer" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="question-137-answer">
				Need last calendar year&#x27;s tax payment amounts for your federal tax filing?
			</button>
		</h3>
		<div id="question-137-answer" class="accordion-collapse collapse show">
			<div class="accordion-body">
				<div class="accordion-text redesign-text fr-view"><p data-pasted="true"><strong>Need copies of Excise, Property Tax, or Water/Sewer AMOUNTS to use to file your State and/or Federal tax return? &nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>We prefer to provide you the amounts in writing, so we can provide you a summary of tax payments that you require for Tax purposes. &nbsp;You can email us at <a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#c9bdbbaca8babcbbacbb89baa8bcaebcbae4a4a8e7aea6bf"><span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="f98d8b9c988a8c8b9c8bb98a988c9e8c8ad49498d79e968f">[email&#160;protected]</span></a> or contact the Treasurer/Collector&apos;s Office at 781-231-4135. &nbsp;Please allow up to 24 hours for your reports to be emailed back to you.</p><p><strong>No printer at home?&nbsp;</strong> We can provide you a copy by mail and we will send out to you via USPS to the property address on file, or to another address you specify.</p><p><strong>Property Tax Amounts&nbsp;</strong>- you will need to provide your property address, your parcel ID, or your account #</p><p><strong>Water/Sewer Utility Amounts -&nbsp;</strong>you will need to provide your property address, your parcel ID, or your account #</p><p><strong>Excise Tax -</strong> IS NOT PUBLIC RECORD - to request these documents you must provide the MA Driver&apos;s License # that the vehicle(s) are registered under, or the plate#(s) for EACH VEHICLE you need. If the vehicle is a leased vehicle - you can contact your leasing company for the amount you paid, or supply the plate# (only) for us to look up for you.</p></div>
				<span class="badge text-body category-pill rounded-pill mw-100 text-truncate">Treasurer / Collector</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</li>
                


<li id="question-136" class="list-group-item accordion px-0 pt-3 faq-question-item edit-container"
	data-subcategory-id="">
	<div class="position-relative accordion-item shadow-sm">
		<h3 class="accordion-header faq-question-header">
			<button class="accordion-button gap-3 z-0" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
				data-bs-target="#question-136-answer" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="question-136-answer">
				WHAT IS A TAX LIEN - Tax Taking and why must it be advertised?
			</button>
		</h3>
		<div id="question-136-answer" class="accordion-collapse collapse show">
			<div class="accordion-body">
				<div class="accordion-text redesign-text fr-view"><p>People who own real property have to pay property taxes. The government uses the money that these taxes generate to pay for schools, public services, libraries, roads, parks, and the like. Typically, the tax amount is based on a property's assessed value. If you have a mortgage on your home, the loan servicer might collect money from you as part of the monthly mortgage payment to later pay the property taxes. The servicer pays the taxes on the homeowner's behalf through an escrow account. But if the taxes aren't collected and paid through this kind of account, the homeowner must pay them directly.</p><p>When homeowners don't pay their property taxes, the overdue amount becomes a lien on the property. A lien is a claim against your property to ensure you'll pay the debt; it effectively makes the property act as collateral for the debt. All states have laws that allow the local government to sell a home through a tax sale process to collect delinquent taxes. Accordingly, homeowners in Massachusetts who fail to make timely payments of property taxes could lose their home after a tax taking. &nbsp;In most cases, you'll get some time after the taking to get current on the overdue amounts before losing ownership of the property.</p><p>Why must it be advertised in the paper? &nbsp; It is a requirement of MA State Law - Tax Takings in Massachusetts - Before a taking, the collector must give you at least a 14-day advance notice by serving it on you or publishing it and post the notice in two or more public places. (<a href="https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleIX/Chapter60/Section53">Mass. Gen. Laws ch. 60, § 53</a>). The city or town then gets title to the property, subject to your right of redemption. (<a href="https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleIX/Chapter60/Section64">Mass. Gen. Laws ch. 60, § 64</a>).</p></div>
				<span class="badge text-body category-pill rounded-pill mw-100 text-truncate">Treasurer / Collector</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</li>
                


<li id="question-105" class="list-group-item accordion px-0 pt-3 faq-question-item edit-container"
	data-subcategory-id="">
	<div class="position-relative accordion-item shadow-sm">
		<h3 class="accordion-header faq-question-header">
			<button class="accordion-button gap-3 z-0" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
				data-bs-target="#question-105-answer" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="question-105-answer">
				What do I do if I have received a Parking Ticket? You can either PAY the parking ticket or APPEAL the parking ticket.
			</button>
		</h3>
		<div id="question-105-answer" class="accordion-collapse collapse show">
			<div class="accordion-body">
				<div class="accordion-text redesign-text fr-view"><p><strong>2 options</strong> for parking ticket appeals as shown in the following</p><ol><li><strong>In Writing - Mail Payments and Appeals to:</strong> <strong>&nbsp;Town of Saugus - 298 Central Street, Suite 5, Saugus, MA 01906</strong><strong>&nbsp;-&nbsp;</strong>You can make a hand written appeal. The ticket will be placed on hold until the next review/hearing date - and you will be notified by mail and/or email with the Hearing Officer&apos;s resolution. Your appeal must be received within 21 days of the ticket date. You can mail your appeal letter with a copy of your ticket - and a copy of your HP placard (if applicable) to Town of Saugus, Parking Hearings Officer, 298 Central Street, Suite 5, Saugus, MA 01906. Forms are available at the Treasurer&apos;s office or <a href="/DocumentCenter/View/1497" target="_blank" rel="noopener">see document link -</a> if you wish to fill out a written appeal immediately.</li><li><strong>Online - Online Payments and Appeals Can Be Done at:</strong><strong>&nbsp;</strong>You can <a href="https://saugusma.rmcpay.com/">make an online appeal</a> - using the web address on the ticket <strong><a href="https://saugusma.rmcpay.com/"></a>-&nbsp;</strong> click &quot;Appeal&quot;. You can upload pictures and/or a detailed letter to go with your appeal. The ticket will be placed on hold until the next review / hearing date - and you will be notified by email with the Hearing Officer&apos;s resolution. <strong>Note -&nbsp;</strong>If you make an appeal online - you will also receive a <strong>mailed</strong> response.<strong>&nbsp;</strong></li></ol><p>If you enter the appeal online, and then send in writing - the appeal received <strong>first</strong> will be taken. It will not be reviewed again.</p><p>If you are not satisfied with your appeal result, you must request a Judicial Court Review at the Essex Superior Court to further appeal. A filing fee will apply to submit a Judicial Court Review. You will have to call the courthouse to inquire as to the cost. Once the Town of Saugus Hearings Officer has made his decision, he will not review again.</p><p><br></p></div>
				<span class="badge text-body category-pill rounded-pill mw-100 text-truncate">Treasurer / Collector</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</li>
                


<li id="question-106" class="list-group-item accordion px-0 pt-3 faq-question-item edit-container"
	data-subcategory-id="">
	<div class="position-relative accordion-item shadow-sm">
		<h3 class="accordion-header faq-question-header">
			<button class="accordion-button gap-3 z-0" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
				data-bs-target="#question-106-answer" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="question-106-answer">
				How is the amount of my Motor Vehicle &amp; Trailer Excise Tax determined?
			</button>
		</h3>
		<div id="question-106-answer" class="accordion-collapse collapse show">
			<div class="accordion-body">
				<div class="accordion-text redesign-text fr-view"><p>To Contact the Mass.gov Customer Service - Excise Division - please call 857-368-8180</p><p>For a guide from the Secretary of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts in regards to penalty and interest calculations; abatements; excise calculations; etc - go to the <a href="https://www.mass.gov/guides/motor-vehicle-excise#-calculating-the-excise-">Mass.gov webpage</a>.</p><p>To Contact the Mass.gov Customer Service, Excise Division - please call 857-368-8180</p><p>The amount of the Motor Vehicle and Trailer Excise Tax on any particular vehicle or trailer is calculated by multiplying the discounted value<strong>**&nbsp;</strong>of the vehicle by the Excise Tax Rate of $25 per $1,000 of value. The $25 Excise Tax Rate applies to all communities in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts.</p><p>It is important to note that, under Massachusetts General Law, the minimum Excise Tax is $5.</p><p>The discounted value of your vehicle is determined by multiplying the Manufacturer&apos;s Suggested Retail Price (MSRP) of your vehicle, when it was originally sold by the manufacturer, by the applicable discount rate as shown as follows.</p><ul><li>In the year preceding the model year of vehicle 50%</li><li>In the model year of vehicle 90%</li><li>One year after model year of vehicle 60%</li><li>Two years after model year of vehicle 40%</li><li>Three years after model year of vehicle 25%</li><li>Four or more years after model year of vehicle 10%</li></ul></div>
				<span class="badge text-body category-pill rounded-pill mw-100 text-truncate">Treasurer / Collector</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</li>
                


<li id="question-138" class="list-group-item accordion px-0 pt-3 faq-question-item edit-container"
